# Server-controlled player physics

[Русский](../ru/server-player-physics.md) · [Documentation index](README.md) · [Gameplay](gameplay.md) · [Host interfaces](host-interfaces.md)

## 1. Scope

This guide documents the source-backed movement/physics slice for **connection-free runtime-owned server players** created through `IServerPlayerOperations`.

It does **not** describe the complete movement model for network-connected Terraria clients. Network player movement still has its own ingress/commit/normalization path and future anti-cheat/tolerance work. The server-player physics described here is a separate authoritative actor path that already advances its own position and velocity inside the TerraRuntime game tick.

The behavioral baseline is TerrariaServer `1.4.5.8`. Only the verified ordinary, unmounted slice is claimed.

## 2. Ownership and data flow

A trusted host submits semantic control intent. It never writes final velocity or position directly.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Host["Trusted host module"] --> API["IServerPlayerOperations"]
    API --> Queue["Bounded authoritative command queue"]
    Queue --> Commands["RuntimeServerPlayerOwner"]
    Commands --> Intent["Generation-bound control intent"]
    Intent --> Tick["ServerRuntimeState.Tick"]
    Tick --> Physics["VanillaServerPlayerDryPhysicsStepper"]
    Physics --> State["RuntimeServerPlayerStateStore"]
```

The game-loop thread remains the mutable-state owner. The host can create/despawn an actor and report buttons/intents; TerraRuntime owns acceleration, jump duration, gravity, collision, slope/step handling, liquid displacement and the final motion state.

## 3. Identity and lifetime

A server-controlled player has two different identities:

- `ServerPlayerId` — stable host-facing logical identity;
- `PlayerHandle` — live generation-safe player-slot identity.

Creation reserves a normal Terraria player slot from the same shared generation-safe pool used by the runtime. A live slot lease is held for the whole actor lifetime.

Horizontal intent, jump intent and internal jump state are keyed by the exact `PlayerHandle`, including its generation. Despawn clears all three and disposes the lease. Reusing the numeric slot therefore cannot transfer stale input to a replacement actor.

## 4. Host API

`IServerPlayerOperations` currently exposes:

```csharp
ValueTask<ServerPlayerCreateResult> CreateAsync(
    ServerPlayerId id,
    float positionX,
    float positionY,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);

ValueTask<bool> SetHorizontalIntentAsync(
    ServerPlayerId id,
    ServerPlayerHorizontalIntent intent,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);

ValueTask<bool> SetJumpIntentAsync(
    ServerPlayerId id,
    ServerPlayerJumpIntent intent,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);

ValueTask<bool> DespawnAsync(
    ServerPlayerId id,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
```

`CreateAsync` reports one of:

- `Created`;
- `InvalidId`;
- `InvalidPosition`;
- `AlreadyExists`;
- `NoAvailableSlot`;
- `QueueRejected`.

The operations facade posts mutations through the authoritative queue. Cancellation is checked before queue admission. Once a control mutation has been accepted into the queue, completion is intentionally not cancellable: TerraRuntime avoids a caller observing an ambiguous “maybe applied” result after the command has become authoritative work.

## 5. Semantic control intents

Horizontal input is deliberately tiny:

| `ServerPlayerHorizontalIntent` | Value | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `Left` | `-1` | hold ordinary left movement |
| `Stop` | `0` | no horizontal movement button |
| `Right` | `1` | hold ordinary right movement |

Jump input models button state rather than a requested velocity:

| `ServerPlayerJumpIntent` | Value | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `Released` | `0` | jump button released |
| `Held` | `1` | jump button held |

`Stop` and `Released` are also the defaults when no explicit intent is stored.

## 6. Tick integration

Server-player physics is enabled when the runtime has both a `RuntimeServerPlayerStateStore` and world tiles. `ServerRuntimeState.Tick()` advances the server-player slice before NPC AI.

For each current server-player snapshot, the runtime:

1. resolves horizontal intent, defaulting to `Stop`;
2. resolves jump intent, defaulting to `Released`;
3. resolves internal jump state, defaulting to `VanillaServerPlayerJumpState.Initial`;
4. runs one `VanillaServerPlayerDryPhysicsStepper` step;
5. commits the resulting jump state;
6. writes motion only when position or velocity actually changed.

A no-op grounded collision therefore does not create a meaningless state revision. Tests pin this behavior.

## 7. Verified step order

The ordinary verified step is not a collection of independent equations. Ordering is part of the behavioral contract.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot["Player snapshot"] --> H["Horizontal control"]
    H --> J["Jump control"]
    J --> L["Read liquid contacts / movement scale"]
    L --> G["Gravity + fall-speed clamp"]
    G --> DownSlope["WalkDownSlope"]
    DownSlope --> StepDown{"velocityY == gravity?"}
    StepDown -->|yes| SD["StepDown"]
    StepDown -->|no| StepUpGate{"velocityY >= gravity?"}
    SD --> StepUpGate
    StepUpGate -->|yes| SU["StepUp"]
    StepUpGate -->|no| Collision["TileCollision"]
    SU --> Collision
    Collision --> Ceiling["Ceiling hit may end active jump"]
    Ceiling --> Disp["Resolve liquid displacement"]
    Disp --> Position["Advance position"]
    Position --> Slope["Post-move slope collision"]
    Slope --> Result["Authoritative motion result"]
```

Changing this order merely because another ordering looks cleaner can change observable motion.

## 8. Source-backed baseline constants

The current ordinary slice pins these TerrariaServer `1.4.5.8` values:

| Quantity | Value | Notes |
|---|---:|---|
| base player hitbox | $20 \times 42\,\mathrm{px}$ | width × height |
| gravity | $0.4\,\mathrm{px/tick^2}$ | added before collision |
| maximum downward speed | $10\,\mathrm{px/tick}$ | fall-speed clamp |
| ordinary run-speed threshold | $3\,\mathrm{px/tick}$ | threshold, **not** a universal hard clamp |
| run acceleration | $0.08\,\mathrm{px/tick^2}$ | ordinary left/right baseline |
| grounded run slowdown | $0.2\,\mathrm{px/tick^2}$ | ordinary stop/reversal path |
| airborne run slowdown | $0.1\,\mathrm{px/tick^2}$ | half grounded slowdown |
| jump vertical speed | $-5.01\,\mathrm{px/tick}$ | negative Y is upward |
| jump counter | $15\,\mathrm{ticks}$ | ordinary held-jump duration state |

The run-speed value is intentionally documented as a threshold. The verified ordinary path may cross it during one acceleration step; for example, the regression suite pins $2.99 \rightarrow 3.07\,\mathrm{px/tick}$ rather than inventing a generic clamp.

## 9. Horizontal control

The current baseline models ordinary unmounted, no-track-boost horizontal movement.

When moving left or right below the corresponding run-speed threshold, the runtime applies reversal slowdown where required and then applies the source-backed acceleration. When there is no active directional acceleration path, horizontal velocity decays using grounded or airborne slowdown.

The distinction between grounded and airborne slowdown is currently derived from vertical velocity in this verified slice: zero vertical velocity uses the full slowdown, non-zero vertical velocity uses half.

Unknown enum values are rejected rather than interpreted as a direction.

## 10. Jump state and release gating

Jump behavior has runtime-owned state:

- `RemainingTicks` mirrors the ordinary vanilla jump counter;
- `ReleaseReady` mirrors the release gate required before a fresh jump may start.

A grounded actor in the initial released state starts a jump when `Held` is applied. While the jump is active and still held, the runtime reasserts the source-backed upward speed and decrements the counter.

Releasing jump cancels the remaining counter and rearms the release gate. Landing while jump remains held does **not** immediately start another jump. A ceiling collision can clear an active remaining jump counter.

The host never manipulates this internal jump state directly.

## 11. Tile, step and slope collision

The baseline uses the verified $20 \times 42\,\mathrm{px}$ player hitbox for world collision.

The current path contains:

- walk-down-slope handling before ordinary tile collision;
- ordinary `StepDown` when the vertical velocity equals baseline gravity;
- ordinary `StepUp` when vertical velocity is at least baseline gravity;
- tile collision for floor, ceiling and walls;
- post-position slope collision.

Regression tests cover floor/wall clamps and ordinary $16\,\mathrm{px}$ step-up/step-down cases. `StepUp` is not applied while the actor is rising faster than baseline gravity.

## 12. Liquid displacement

Liquid contact currently changes **position displacement**, not the persisted collision velocity.

| Contact | Movement scale |
|---|---:|
| water | $0.5$ |
| lava | $0.5$ |
| honey | $0.25$ |
| shimmer | $0.375$ |
| no supported liquid contact | $1$ |

If tile collision changes one velocity axis, that clamped axis advances without the liquid movement scale; an unchanged axis keeps the scale. This prevents a collision clamp from being incorrectly diluted by water/honey/shimmer movement scaling.

This support does **not** imply complete vanilla swimming/floating/jump/gravity behavior in liquids.

## 13. Validation and failure behavior

Creation rejects an unassigned `ServerPlayerId`, non-finite spawn coordinates, duplicates and exhausted player-slot capacity.

Control commands reject an unknown/unassigned actor and invalid intent enum values.

The dry-physics step refuses to advance:

- an unassigned player handle;
- a dead player;
- a mounted player;
- non-finite position or velocity input.

A rejected/out-of-slice step is not replaced by guessed motion.

## 14. Verified slice versus remaining work

### In the current verified server-player slice

- connection-free runtime-owned player lifecycle;
- generation-safe slot ownership and control state;
- semantic horizontal and jump intents;
- ordinary left/right acceleration and slowdown;
- ordinary release-gated jump control;
- baseline gravity and fall-speed clamp;
- base player hitbox collision;
- walk-down-slope;
- ordinary step-up and step-down;
- tile and post-move slope collision;
- water/lava/honey/shimmer displacement scaling;
- runtime-tick integration and revision-preserving no-op behavior.

### Explicitly outside the current slice

- treating this subsystem as the anti-cheat/physics authority for ordinary network-connected players;
- dash families;
- mounts;
- grapples;
- auto-jump and extra-jump families;
- accessories that change movement/jump behavior;
- minecart/track boosts;
- wind and sandstorm movement branches;
- wrong-ground and portal movement branches;
- the remaining liquid-specific jump, gravity and floating rules;
- broad exceptional movement such as teleports/respawn transitions.

The unsupported list is a contract boundary, not a queue of behaviors to approximate.

## 15. Evidence

Current regression evidence includes:

- `ServerRuntimeServerPlayerPhysicsTests` — proves authoritative `Tick()` advances a connection-free actor and does not increment revision for a true no-op;
- `VanillaServerPlayerDryPhysicsStepperTests` — gravity, fall-speed clamp, floor/wall collision, liquid displacement and baseline constants;
- `VanillaServerPlayerHorizontalControlTests` — acceleration, reversal, grounded/airborne slowdown and the non-hard-clamp run-speed edge;
- `VanillaServerPlayerJumpControlTests` — start/hold/release/rearm behavior and invalid intent rejection;
- `VanillaServerPlayerStepPhysicsTests` — ordinary step-up/step-down and rising-step exclusion;
- runtime operation/intent tests — command queue, host operations, generation-safe lifecycle and semantic intent behavior.

These tests prove the implemented slice. They do not elevate the out-of-scope branches above to verified vanilla behavior.
